WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.150
In this place so unreal

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.820
I wait for dawn to come

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:12.950
Farther and farther, like the sky I saw that day

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.210
What a joke this hope is

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.750
I acted as if I had forgotten

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.260
Farther and farther, like the dream I saw that day

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.140
I just want to know the meaning of it

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.270
Within this scattered time

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.100
Along with the breath of ours

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.070
And of this pain that never goes away

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.570
Even though the roaring of the sea muffles the voices

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.490
Darkness engulfs everything ahead

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.490
The light that passes through our fragile hearts

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.910
Won't go away

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.750
We only relied on our compass

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.880
We believed you

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:14.720
While we stand here

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.890
We're searching for the unseen future

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:29.860
Zipang

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.530
- Hard starboard.
- Hard starboard!

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.560
The battleship... Yamato?

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.860
Midway

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.490
The Yamato? But how?

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.250
Yamato... pardon me.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.620
The unidentified ship is signaling.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.500
What should we do, sir?

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.380
No need to reply to a ship that's refusing radio contact.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.420
Until we understand our situation, it's best to avoid them.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.800
Understood.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.720
Starboard with linear velocity.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.270
What's this?

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.570
There's no doubt about it. It's the Yamato!

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.330
There's a large vessel. Distance, approximately one mile.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.330
It appears to be a Nagato Class.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.800
First we see the Yamato? Now a Nagato?!

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.300
I-I know this formation...

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.930
Got them.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.390
CIC to bridge. Two targets approaching from left and right.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:32.770
Distance 2500. Our course is being blocked off.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.190
I won't allow any firing.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.690
Well then, we'll just have to go straight up the middle.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.610
Can you do it?

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:41.900
Look at the smoke...

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.410
Those boiler warships can't possibly match our acceleration.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.620
Attention all hands!

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.660
We'll set up a feint, then run for it.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.380
Rudder 10 degrees starboard! Advance with slow speed!

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.850
The right target is turning starboard! Distance 300!

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:07.350
The door's opening up!

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.560
Rudder, 10 degrees left! Break through at flanking speed now!

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.250
No anti-aircraft, anti-submarine, and anti-surface targets.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.630
We are pulling away from the unknown fleet.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:41.920
Distance 10,000 yards.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.190
Aurora in the south seas. No satellite communications.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.600
No lost warships.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.190
On top of that, a mysterious fleet appears.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.820
I don't know, something way beyond our comprehension has happened here.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.530
Mysterious? It's no mystery that it was the Yamato back there.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:11.040
You saw those 46 centimeter gun turrets!

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.790
What now, Oguri? You're saying we saw a ghost ship?

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.250
A ghost ship wouldn't have sent us a signal now, would it?

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:22.960
Not only that, I saw the destroyer's crew surprised with our acceleration.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:25.930
They sure ain't no ghosts.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.510
In 1945, the Imperial Navy's battleship Yamato...

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.770
...was sunk and still rests at the bottom of the sea on the Kagoshima coast.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.600
That's just a fact.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.020
Maybe Hollywood is making one of their Blockbuster movies out here?

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.480
Then they would have exposed themselves to us.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:42.610
They would have identified themselves.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.610
Have we got any experts on the Japanese Imperial Navy onboard?

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.830
Our Senior Chief Petty Officer Yanagi is a military history fanatic.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.200
- Officer Yanagi!
- Yes!

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.830
There's no doubt about it.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.170
That was the former Imperial Navy's Combined Fleet,

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.090
and that formation was a war arrangement from June the Fourth,

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:07.300
...just before the Battle of Midway.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.010
The Battle of Midway?

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:11.600
Yes.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:17.730
Besides the battleship Yamato, I recognized the Nagato and the Mutsu.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.310
The other ships I spotted were most likely...

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:24.030
...the Fuso, Yamashiro, Ise and the Hyuga battleships.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.860
That was the Strike Force for the capture of Midway Island.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.030
Supporting the First Carrier Strike Force under Vice Admiral Nagumo.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.540
They were led by Commander in Chief Isoroku Yamamoto.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.120
The problem is, why has the Japanese Imperial fleet...

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.540
...reappeared after 60 years?

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.340
If the situation has changed, we should have been called back to port, right?

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.050
That's right. This isn't just a drill anymore...

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.720
We've lost sight of the consort ships. Right now, our priority...

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.060
...is to search for the lost ships, and confirm our safety.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:58.850
Yes, sir!

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:01.350
If what Petty Officer Yanagi said is correct,

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.440
that means the former Imperial Navy fleet...

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.030
...has just reappeared in the Pacific Ocean.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.280
I wonder why.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.280
I'm hoping that's it. And not the other way round...

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.280
The other way round? What do you mean?

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:15.370
It's the 4th of June.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.830
Do you remember looking at the moon last night?

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.250
It was a full moon. So what about it?

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.340
In just one night, how much would a full moon change?

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.510
Half moon?

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.890
In short... I don't think the Imperial Navy reappeared.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.430
Instead, I think we've appeared...

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.810
...in the Battle of Midway.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.070
Captain, your orders.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.700
Since we've received no change of orders from the headquarters,

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:54.410
we will proceed as planned,

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.830
and stay on our present course until we reach Pearl Harbor.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.330
Understood!

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.540
All hands, stay sharp and keep your eyes peeled.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.300
If this ship has really traveled through time,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.600
what's waiting for us ahead... is a battlefield.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.560
Radar contact!

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.980
Unidentified aircraft approaching.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.690
35 degrees starboard. Distance 20 nautical miles.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:11.780
Speed 100 knots. Altitude 200.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.820
It's following a path above our warship.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:18.030
Estimated time is from 06:10 to 06:20.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:20.530
- Get me confirmation!
- Yes, sir!

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.870
If it's cruising at 100 knots, it's not likely a missile or a jet.

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:26.540
- Keep your eye on it.
- Aye-aye!

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.210
Display the image! Bring it up on the front monitor!

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.470
Zoom in full! Enhance the image!

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:40.140
It's on fire. I think it's been hit.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.220
It's a...

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.100
...Dauntless.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.020
Dauntless?! Isn't that a World War Two aircraft?

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:50.270
Guard! Visual confirmation?

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.610
Aircraft sighted! Dead ahead!

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:58.780
Chances of an attack are unlikely, but we must be prepared for anything.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:01.370
Navigator, will it clear us?

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:02.990
We've got it, sir.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.620
- Rudder 20 degrees port!
- Rudder 20 degrees port!

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:22.930
Aircraft has hit water! 140 degrees starboard! Distance 150!

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.430
Assess the perimeter! Do you see any survivors?

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:32.060
It's going down fast! Nothing but oil on the surface!

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.190
This... This is not a dream.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:39.530
Signal Officer, because... I'm bleeding here...

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.990
Y-You fool! Hurry up and get to the sick-bay!

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.700
Senior Chief Petty Officer Yanagi, hit by shrapnel!

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.290
CIC to bridge, 90 degrees port. Distance 50 miles.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:55.210
About 20 ships. They're moving in circles, whilst exchanging radio signals.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:59.220
Content of exchange is encrypted and analysis is not possible.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.180
In the sky above sea, about 100 targets!

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.060
Give me the ship's mic.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.520
This is Captain Umezu speaking.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:19.610
Right now, as planned, we are on a heading for Pearl Harbor.

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:26.290
Since last night, several strange anomalies have occurred.

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:31.500
After an objective analysis of the situation, unbelievable as it may seem,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:35.500
it appears that this ship has entered the Battle of Midway,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.510
that puts us back in time to June 5th, 1942.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:49.850
Our top priority now is to find and rejoin the lost escorts.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.560
We are now approaching the air battlefield region.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.650
I'm asking you, until further notice,

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:58.530
just hold your positions and do your duties.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:03.740
Let's pull together, be professional, and above all, remain calm.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:05.200
That's all.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:12.580
In a bizarre situation like this, how are we supposed to act?

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:19.880
To have arrived on this day during the Battle of Midway,

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.720
it's too much of a coincidence. No...

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:27.010
To seek for a meaning in reality is too dangerous.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.060
Hey, is this thing called the "time slip"?

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:32.060
That's impossible...

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:37.270
Just as I thought. It really was the Yamato.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:41.820
Director, when was the Battle of Midway?

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.530
June 5th, 1942.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.780
But why did we travel back in time 60 years?

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.740
What's going to happen? To all of us?!

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:53.660
I don't know, but people are hungry and I have to cook them some food!

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:56.330
The chicken is going to burn, you idiot!

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:57.460
Got it!

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.840
Distance to the battle region, 25 nautical miles.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.550
Scan for Anti-Aircraft, Anti-Sub and Antisurface. Stay sharp!

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.600
I got something!

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.820
Up on the horizon, there's a large quantity of black smoke.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.740
It's an anti-aircraft barrage.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.530
Executive Officer, what time is it?

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:27.360
It's 0718, Sir.

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.490
5 more minutes.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.540
What did you say?

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:34.960
It's the zero hour, 0723.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:38.380
The US bombers from Midway...

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:42.550
...were badly ambushed by the experienced Zero Fighters.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.840
However, while the Zero Fighters were still flying low...

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.800
...along the water's surface,

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:55.180
the Dauntless dive bombers used this opening to destroy the Imperial Navy.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:58.810
It is the devil's 0723.

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:03.610
Aircraft carriers Kaga, Soryu and Akagi were mercilessly attacked.

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:15.450
250 kilograms.

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:18.830
The Kaga swallowed up the four direct hits by the dive bombers.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.250
Following that, Akagi. Two 500 kilogram bombs direct hits.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.180
Soryu was converged on by 17 planes,

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.970
and took three direct hits.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.480
One after the other, the bombshells exploded.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.190
And within the blink of an eye,

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.020
the three warships were completely engulfed in flames.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:49.410
And here we are, right in the middle of the Battle of Midway.

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:51.530
I can't stay here.

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:53.490
Ah, wait...

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.960
I have to see it, with my own eyes.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.750
Weapons officer, to the bridge.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:02.380
Aye-aye!

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.400
Captain! What should we do?!

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:24.270
Are we just gonna sit here and watch like it's a cable special?

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:26.400
We should jump in.

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:27.900
Jump in?

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.660
Are you saying we should join the battle?

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:35.450
There are Japanese soldiers dying out there. They're our ancestors!

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:39.410
If we take down even one plane, hundreds of lives could be saved!

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:44.380
And then we set off a butterfly effect.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.920
Butterfly... what?

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.300
In Peking, if one extra butterfly...

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:54.180
...is able to flap its wings, it could cause a storm in New York a month later.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:59.600
Even a micro phenomenon could have a big effect on the macro scale.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:02.900
What's more, this ship is capable of sinking even a nuclear submarine.

00:16:03.100 --> 00:16:07.570
We are capable of taking on the entire US strike force in this era.

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:10.280
It's not just about butterflies anymore,

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.700
but a weapon 60 years beyond this time.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:16.200
A single action from us could have a huge impact on the future.

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.620
Even if we do return to the 21st century,

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:21.750
the world might have become a totally different place.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:26.590
We stand as a serious threat to history right now.

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:31.090
Then... what the heck are we doing here?

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:34.720
Look, Kikuchi! It's a war out there!

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:39.600
And this is a war ship. What better way to test a warship than to go to war!

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:42.940
Calm yourself down. This is a coincidence.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:45.600
There is no meaning in coincidence. None.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:49.780
Coincidence? You're saying this is nothing but coincidence?

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:52.780
Executive Officer.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.320
How do you feel about all of this?

00:16:55.320 --> 00:16:56.990
It makes me angry.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.990
Setting up tactics with conceit and arrogance.

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.960
Just thinking of the ships, fighter planes,

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:08.880
and all of the elite pilots and sailors who were lost in the battle.

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:12.510
Thinking about the loss of those men is tearing me up inside!

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:20.640
However, our mission right now is to find our lost fleet,

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:25.020
and to bring Mirai and her 241 crew members back home safely.

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:28.110
Weapons officer, Navigator, and everyone else...

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:31.020
...should keep this in mind before making any moves.

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.190
Communication Room.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:35.530
We've caught a broadcast from Honolulu.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:39.410
The first report of the Battle of Midway is being made.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:44.200
Franklin D. Roosevelt's encouragement speech to the soldiers is being played.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:48.790
So there it is.

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:52.500
I'm guessing we won't be getting a warm welcome at Pearl Harbor.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:54.800
Besides running out of provisions,

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:57.680
if we proceed any further into the battle zone,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:01.560
this ship and her entire crew will be in danger.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.060
I don't know what the situation is at Yokosuka,

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:08.060
but maybe this phenomenon is limited to our current location.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.480
While searching for our lost escort ships,

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:12.980
we will retrace our previous route.

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.070
Change our destination to Yokosuka Base.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:19.160
Yes, sir. Heading to 270 degrees.

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:21.700
- 30 degrees starboard!
- 30 degrees starboard!

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.080
- Exec Officer, the charts please.
- Yes, sir.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.580
Since we can't use satellite,

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:30.130
we'll have to rely on the gyrocompass and astrononomical observation.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:40.510
The Akagi, Kaga and Soryu are all burning.

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:00.990
Weapons Officer, can you tell me why we're turning back?

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:02.320
It's an order from the Captain.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:06.580
When those three aircraft carriers go down, 1881 people will die!

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:09.210
Are we just gonna turn our backs on them?!

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.250
The right to alter the destined course of history...

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:15.170
We don't have that.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.150
Without any orders to follow,

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:38.150
we have no choice but to determine our own course.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:42.070
This journey will definitely be a difficult one.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:51.290
1430 hours.

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:55.380
The one remaining carrier, Hiryu, takes four direct hits.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:20:00.380
Fifteen hours later, it goes down, taking 416 people to the bottom with it.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:13.400
1815. The Soryu sinks.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.810
718 people perish.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:20.530
That's...?!

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:34.250
"Hi No Maru: Symbol of the Sun"

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:34.250
That's an Imperial Navy's seaplane. Hi No Maru confirmed!

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:36.330
Any survivors?

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:39.550
One survivor located in the rear seat.

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:42.550
It's possible he might still be alive.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.050
Captain, I can't overlook this one!

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:49.010
Okay. Assess the situation and report back to me.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:49.810
Yes, sir.

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:56.440
Advance with slow speed. Ready searchlights and rescue boat.

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:59.520
Turn on searchlights!

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:01.740
Lower the boat now!

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:17.830
Hold on, Yosuke.

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:20.460
Aren't you afraid?

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:23.340
You're gonna be messing history with your own hands?

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.380
Don't you think that's conceited and overestimating?

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:30.810
I'm neither a ghost or a butterfly.

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:34.020
I'm here as an SDF officer.

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:45.570
Supply Officer, Medical Officer! Report to left side of the ship now!

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:17.160
Passing through the distant time

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:22.400
Just peacefully

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:34.180
Without losing your way, you walked towards me

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:47.060
How many times have I grasped tightly those small hands of yours?

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:53.700
Not being able to do anything

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:59.770
I kept on praying

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:11.580
Sorrow or agony isn't enough

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:27.300
That's why now as if I wanted to make up... I'm looking at you

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:44.180
I would like to know this ship's allegiance and its mission.

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:46.390
I can only tell you that...

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:50.150
All of us on board, are Japanese, just like you.

00:23:50.150 --> 00:23:52.900
You are Japanese people, but where have you come from?

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:55.690
If I were to tell you that...

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:58.780
You might never be able to get back to your homeland again.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:03.450
You will become a drifter of this Pacific Ocean together with us all!

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:04.870
Next Episode: Drifters

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:09.880
This story is purely fiction. The names of the
characters and organizations that appear have no
connections with actual people and organizations.
